WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.266 --> 00:00:01.167 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Welcome back.
-Thank you.

00:00:01.167 --> 00:00:02.268 align:start size:94% position:6%
-I'm so happy you're here.
-I am, too.

00:00:02.268 --> 00:00:05.839
It's nice to be here.
-This audio memoir is wonderful.

00:00:05.839 --> 00:00:07.307 align:start size:94% position:6%
And one of the great thing
about it is,

00:00:07.307 --> 00:00:08.842 align:start size:94% position:6%
there are certain songs that
as a listener,

00:00:08.842 --> 00:00:10.844 align:start size:94% position:6%
you become so comfortable
with it, you forget,

00:00:10.844 --> 00:00:12.412 align:start size:94% position:6%
at some point,
they weren't songs,

00:00:12.412 --> 00:00:13.513 line:92%
and someone had to write them.

00:00:13.513 --> 00:00:17.484 align:start size:94% position:6%
And you talk about writing
"Carolina in My Mind."

00:00:17.484 --> 00:00:20.153 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it's really
just a wonderful story.

00:00:20.153 --> 00:00:24.591
-Well, yeah, we had hoped people
would be interested in that.

00:00:24.591 --> 00:00:29.262 align:start size:94% position:6%
And it was.
You know, I can't remember

00:00:29.262 --> 00:00:31.164 line:92%
an awful lot of the particular

00:00:31.164 --> 00:00:33.833 align:start size:94% position:6%
of writing
some of those older songs.

00:00:33.833 --> 00:00:36.136 align:start size:94% position:6%
But I do remember "Carolina.
-Yeah.

00:00:36.136 --> 00:00:40.907
And it was -- Can you share with
us some of how it came about

00:00:40.907 --> 00:00:44.277 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, yeah, when I
was recording my first album

00:00:44.277 --> 00:00:48.081
I was the first person signed to
the Beatles' new record label,

00:00:48.081 --> 00:00:51.551 align:start size:94% position:6%
Apple Records.
And they had --

00:00:51.551 --> 00:00:53.853 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Can I just stop real quick?
Like --

00:00:53.853 --> 00:00:54.521 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:00:54.521 --> 00:00:56.289
'Cause I imagine anyone,
when they sign their first

00:00:56.289 --> 00:00:58.725 align:start size:94% position:6%
record deal, there's a lot
of pressure behind it.

00:00:58.725 --> 00:01:03.063 align:start size:94% position:6%
But when it's the Beatles --
I mean, that seems like

00:01:03.063 --> 00:01:06.099 line:92%
there'd be even more pressure.

00:01:06.099 --> 00:01:08.268 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, I don't --
[ Laughter ]

00:01:08.268 --> 00:01:10.437 align:start size:94% position:6%
No, it felt --
it felt very easy.

00:01:10.437 --> 00:01:11.738 align:start size:94% position:6%
-All right.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:11.738 --> 00:01:12.972 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah, okay. Okay.
-No, it did.

00:01:12.972 --> 00:01:16.276
There was very little sort of --
And that may have been

00:01:16.276 --> 00:01:18.478 align:start size:94% position:6%
the problem
with Apple Records, actually

00:01:18.478 --> 00:01:19.245 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:01:19.245 --> 00:01:21.848 align:start size:94% position:6%
-That it was pretty much
hemorrhaging money.

00:01:21.848 --> 00:01:22.715 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:01:22.715 --> 00:01:25.852 align:start size:94% position:6%
But while that window
of opportunity was open, I -

00:01:25.852 --> 00:01:30.223
it was a great thing for myself,
Mary Hopkins, Jackie Lomax

00:01:30.223 --> 00:01:32.459 align:start size:94% position:6%
Billy Preston, you know.
-Yeah, of course.

00:01:32.459 --> 00:01:37.363 size:81% position:41%
-And a number of other
lucky acts who were --

00:01:37.363 --> 00:01:39.365 align:start size:94% position:6%
You know,
but it was only a year or so

00:01:39.365 --> 00:01:44.304
that the label still had other
non-Beatle performers on it.

00:01:44.304 --> 00:01:47.040 align:start size:94% position:6%
But, anyway, we were --
we were --

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:49.209
-I imagine that was a weir
meeting day where they said,

00:01:49.209 --> 00:01:52.011
"You know what? I think we'r
just gonna be the Beatles.

00:01:52.011 --> 00:01:52.612 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:01:52.612 --> 00:01:54.981
"We think our record label
should just be the Beatles

00:01:54.981 --> 00:01:55.915 align:start size:94% position:6%
for here on out."
[ Laughter ]

00:01:55.915 --> 00:01:58.952 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Well, a guy named Allen Klein
who was actually, I think,

00:01:58.952 --> 00:02:03.523 size:84% position:42%
a New Yorker convinced,
I think, John and Yoko

00:02:03.523 --> 00:02:07.026 align:start size:94% position:6%
that he'd be a good manage
for the Beatles.

00:02:07.026 --> 00:02:09.929 align:start size:94% position:6%
This is after
Brian Epstein died.

00:02:09.929 --> 00:02:14.868 align:start size:94% position:6%
And Allen Klein
basically threw everybody

00:02:14.868 --> 00:02:16.503 align:start size:94% position:6%
off the record label
is what happened.

00:02:16.503 --> 00:02:18.972 align:start size:94% position:6%
-But you worked on this song
with the --

00:02:18.972 --> 00:02:21.307
It wasn't just the label.
You actually went and brought it

00:02:21.307 --> 00:02:24.444 align:start size:94% position:6%
to the Beatles and worked on i
with some of the Beatles.

00:02:24.444 --> 00:02:26.913 align:start size:94% position:6%
-With that particular song
well, it was --

00:02:26.913 --> 00:02:29.549 align:start size:94% position:6%
I wrote it
while we were recording.

00:02:29.549 --> 00:02:31.050 align:start size:94% position:6%
We took a break.
Well, that is to say,

00:02:31.050 --> 00:02:36.422
the Beatles took a break, so
the studio that they were using,

00:02:36.422 --> 00:02:38.224 align:start size:94% position:6%
when they weren't there,
I was there.

00:02:38.224 --> 00:02:41.594 align:start size:94% position:6%
I would sort of scamper in
there, you know,

00:02:41.594 --> 00:02:43.530 align:start size:94% position:6%
and put down a few
of my little tracks

00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:46.266 align:start size:94% position:6%
and then clear out
before they came back.

00:02:46.266 --> 00:02:46.799 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Laughter ]

00:02:46.799 --> 00:02:48.868 align:start size:94% position:6%
But I got to hang around
a little bit, too.

00:02:48.868 --> 00:02:51.437 align:start size:94% position:6%
It was great.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:51.437 --> 00:02:52.972 line:92%
-In the moment, did you know -

00:02:52.972 --> 00:02:55.241 align:start size:94% position:6%
I mean, you know,
did you know it was great?

00:02:55.241 --> 00:02:57.644
Or was it one of those --
"Okay, yeah, okay, good"?

00:02:57.644 --> 00:02:58.411 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:02:58.411 --> 00:03:01.314 align:start size:94% position:6%
-"I'm glad to hear that."
-Yeah, I knew it --

00:03:01.314 --> 00:03:04.117 align:start size:94% position:6%
I knew it was extraordinar
and exceptional,

00:03:04.117 --> 00:03:06.619 line:92%
and I couldn't believe it.

00:03:06.619 --> 00:03:07.554 size:81% position:41% line:92%
But they took a break.

00:03:07.554 --> 00:03:10.023 align:start size:94% position:6%
So they basically
closed the studio down.

00:03:10.023 --> 00:03:13.393 size:84% position:42%
And I went to an island
in the Mediterranean

00:03:13.393 --> 00:03:17.230 align:start size:94% position:6%
off the coast of Spain,
called Formentera.

00:03:17.230 --> 00:03:19.732 align:start size:94% position:6%
Formentera.
And while I was there --

00:03:19.732 --> 00:03:24.270
in those days, it was an
extremely sort of laid-bac

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:25.905 size:75% position:38% line:92%
and primitive place.

00:03:25.905 --> 00:03:29.342
There were no -- There reall
were no cars on the island

00:03:29.342 --> 00:03:34.647 align:start size:94% position:6%
And people got around
on bicycles or on their feet

00:03:34.647 --> 00:03:38.818 size:81% position:41% line:92%
And I just -- I met --

00:03:38.818 --> 00:03:41.754 align:start size:94% position:6%
I met some wonderful peopl
while I was there

00:03:41.754 --> 00:03:43.289 size:88% position:44% line:92%
and had some adventures.

00:03:43.289 --> 00:03:46.092 align:start size:94% position:6%
But myself --
[ Laughter ]

00:03:46.092 --> 00:03:50.296 align:start size:94% position:6%
Myself -- excellent.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:50.296 --> 00:03:55.768
But myself and a girl name
Karen from Sweden, we --

00:03:55.768 --> 00:03:59.072 align:start size:94% position:6%
we went to -- we went to
a neighboring island.

00:03:59.072 --> 00:04:00.607 align:start size:94% position:6%
There was a ferry service
over there.

00:04:00.607 --> 00:04:02.408 align:start size:94% position:6%
We went over to see
what was happening

00:04:02.408 --> 00:04:05.144 align:start size:94% position:6%
on the island of Ibiza.
-All right.

00:04:05.144 --> 00:04:06.179 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:04:06.179 --> 00:04:09.649 align:start size:94% position:6%
And while we were there,
we went to --

00:04:09.649 --> 00:04:12.252 align:start size:94% position:6%
I can't remember if it was
a wedding or a funeral.

00:04:12.252 --> 00:04:15.888
But we went to some sort of a --
[ Laughter ]

00:04:15.888 --> 00:04:19.792 size:84% position:42%
But it was some kind of
congregation of people.

00:04:19.792 --> 00:04:21.961 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.
-People seemed like they wer

00:04:21.961 --> 00:04:23.663 align:start size:94% position:6%
in a good mood.
-So probably wedding.

00:04:23.663 --> 00:04:25.298 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah, well, possibly,
but, you know?

00:04:25.298 --> 00:04:27.400 size:88% position:44%
-Yeah, you never know.
Could've been a bad guy.

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:30.870 align:start size:94% position:6%
Yeah.
-But we --

00:04:30.870 --> 00:04:33.339
You know, we had intended
to take the ferry back ove

00:04:33.339 --> 00:04:38.144 align:start size:94% position:6%
to Formentera by, you know
the evening, by the last boat,

00:04:38.144 --> 00:04:39.145 line:92%
but we missed the last boat.

00:04:39.145 --> 00:04:41.981 align:start size:94% position:6%
We had -- We had our tickets
but we had no money.

00:04:41.981 --> 00:04:45.852
So there was no -- no plac
to get a room or any shelter

00:04:45.852 --> 00:04:47.720 line:92%
We just stayed on the streets.

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:52.091 align:start size:94% position:6%
And there was a cafe that
was closed down for the night.

00:04:52.091 --> 00:04:55.028
And the tables were up -- th
chairs were up on the tables

00:04:55.028 --> 00:04:57.930
you know, like they are.
And we just took a couple

00:04:57.930 --> 00:05:00.633 size:75% position:38%
of those chairs down
and sat at the table

00:05:00.633 --> 00:05:04.871 align:start size:94% position:6%
for hours and hours
while the night went by.

00:05:04.871 --> 00:05:07.307 line:92%
And I started getting homesick

00:05:07.307 --> 00:05:12.512 align:start size:94% position:6%
And I thought of this song
and my home so far away

00:05:12.512 --> 00:05:17.450 align:start size:94% position:6%
and wrote "Carolina in My Mind
as the sun was coming up.

00:05:17.450 --> 00:05:20.320 align:start size:94% position:6%
But we're not gonna
play that song tonight.

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:22.021 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
[ Laughter ]

00:05:22.021 --> 00:05:25.458
-So I'll introduce the son
that we will play tonight

00:05:25.458 --> 00:05:28.428 align:start size:94% position:6%
the next time I'm here.
-Oh, that'd be great, yeah

00:05:28.428 --> 00:05:29.362 align:start size:94% position:6%
[ Laughter ]
-That's right.

00:05:29.362 --> 00:05:32.398 align:start size:94% position:6%
-And then we'll cut it togethe
for the rerun. Yeah.

00:05:32.398 --> 00:05:34.200 align:start size:94% position:6% line:92%
[ Applause ]

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:38.971 align:start size:94% position:6%
Your album -- these are
old standards you're playing

00:05:38.971 --> 00:05:40.406
These are the songs that,
when you were coming up

00:05:40.406 --> 00:05:44.944
and playing music, folk music --
the music that was more modern

00:05:44.944 --> 00:05:46.279 align:start size:94% position:6%
This is the music
your parents listened to.

00:05:46.279 --> 00:05:48.381 size:88% position:44%
Did you like the music
your parents listened to

00:05:48.381 --> 00:05:51.784 align:start size:94% position:6%
when you were young?
-You know, I did -- I did.

00:05:51.784 --> 00:05:55.788 align:start size:94% position:6%
Certainly, when I was a kid,
I loved that music.

00:05:55.788 --> 00:05:57.223 line:92%
I got an attitude about it

00:05:57.223 --> 00:06:01.527 align:start size:94% position:6%
when I was sort of
a teenage musician, myself

00:06:01.527 --> 00:06:06.099 align:start size:94% position:6%
But, you know, I --
these songs taught me a lot.

00:06:06.099 --> 00:06:07.600 align:start size:94% position:6%
-Yeah.
-Just learning to play the

00:06:07.600 --> 00:06:12.972 size:81% position:41%
on the guitar as a kid
taught me the changes

00:06:12.972 --> 00:06:15.942 align:start size:94% position:6%
and taught me a lot
about harmonies.

00:06:15.942 --> 00:06:20.046 align:start size:94% position:6%
And just there's a wealth
of information there.

00:06:20.046 --> 00:06:23.116 align:start size:94% position:6%
And, you know, it seems likely
to me that this is --

00:06:23.116 --> 00:06:28.488
Well, I know it's the case tha
Bob Dylan and Paul McCartney

00:06:28.488 --> 00:06:33.960 align:start size:94% position:6%
and Paul Simon and the entir
generation of songwriters

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:37.563 align:start size:94% position:6%
that I come from was also
informed by this same music,

00:06:37.563 --> 00:06:38.531 align:start size:94% position:6%
these standards.
You know?

00:06:38.531 --> 00:06:40.800 align:start size:94% position:6%
-We're so excited you're gonna
play one for us tonight.

00:06:40.800 --> 00:06:42.935
And it is just always such
a delight to have you here

00:06:42.935 --> 00:06:45.638 align:start size:94% position:6%
Thank you so much.
James Taylor.

